course: dictionary
lesson: 4517
title: Words for things we wear
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Headgear is a noun. It means anything you wear on your head, such as hats, helmets and caps. The word "gear" means things you wear or use for a purpose, so headgear is the gear for your head.
explain: Headgear is uncountable, which means it names a whole group of things. We say "protective headgear" or "wear headgear", not "two headgears". To count the items, name them: "a helmet" or "two caps".
explain: «Helmets and scrum caps help with cuts and some bumps, but no headgear can stop the brain moving inside the skull, so the same rule applies to everyone.» Helmets and scrum caps are examples of headgear. The sentence also tells you that headgear helps with some injuries but cannot do everything. Context clues like this help you understand the whole meaning.
fact: Headgear means anything worn on the head, such as hats, helmets and caps.
fact: In sport, protective headgear is a head covering that helps keep players safer, for example in boxing or rugby.
fact: Headgear can also mean a brace worn on the head to help straighten teeth.
fact: «Helmets and scrum caps help with cuts and some bumps, but no headgear can stop the brain moving inside the skull.» So headgear helps, but it is not a magic shield.
fact: Headgear is uncountable, so you do not add an s to it. Name the item instead, such as "a helmet" or "a cap".
exercise: Which of these is headgear? | A pair of gloves | A school bag | A pair of trainers | *A cycling helmet
exercise: «Helmets and scrum caps help with cuts and some bumps, but no headgear can stop the brain moving inside the skull.» What does this tell you about headgear? | It is only worn in history lessons | *It can protect some things, but not everything | It stops every injury | It is a kind of food
exercise: Which meaning fits a boxer's headgear? | *A protective head covering | A scarf for the neck | A hat for the sun | A brace for straightening teeth
test: Which phrase uses headgear correctly? | *protective headgear | protective headgears | a headgears
test: What is orthodontic headgear used for? | To keep the head warm in winter | To cover the ears in the cold | *To help straighten teeth | To protect the head in rugby
exam: Jayden says headgear only means helmets. Who is right? | Jayden is right, because helmets are the only headgear | Neither is right, because headgear means shoes | *Jayden is wrong, because headgear includes hats, caps and helmets | Jayden is right, but only in history lessons
